2026-10-02.introduction:
Divers working in confined spaces, such as underwater cave systems [ 1 ] , pipelines [ 2 ] , coral reef canyons [ 3 ] , or around unexploded ordnance [ 4 ] , must often maintain a safe operating distance from the surrounding walls or objects.
In the case of cave diving, for example, avoiding cave walls helps divers maintain optimal visibility conditions by avoiding collisions with the surrounding enclosure.
This minimizes stirring up deposited sediment [ 5 ] , which is a leading cause of diver disorientation-induced fatalities in cave diving accidents [ 6 ] .
Maintaining safe standoff distances for divers during coral reef explorations is also important, since ocean currents, other divers, or marine obstacles can inadvertently cause divers to touch coral [ 3 ] , which devastates coral health.

In this paper, we introduce RADMCS ( R obotic A ssisted D iver M ovement in C onfined S paces) with the goal of addressing these distance-maintaining challenges.
RADMCS is an easy-to-deploy wearable robotic solution that assists in maintaining a preset standoff distance from encompassing walls or objects by providing force-feedback to the diver when they get too close to such objects.
The force-feedback mechanism is driven by monocular depth estimation techniques that estimate the distance between the robot’s cameras and the surrounding structure, and the robot uses its thrusters in response to this perceived distance.
Fig. 1 shows the haptic feedback mechanism working in response to the distance between the surrounding reef and the robot’s cameras.
Because the robot is affixed to the diver, the actuation, in turn, perturbs the diver’s movements. RADMCS integrates on the diver’s existing equipment by strapping onto the scuba diving air tank.
This eliminates the need for complex exoskeleton style suits or augmented swimming solutions that require additional training or instrumentation.
It is self-contained and does not interfere with the diver’s natural body movements beyond the perturbative feedback provided to the diver.

Our contributions are the

introduction of a novel, wearable underwater robotic system for movement assistance;

presentation of systematic testing of threshold sensitivity (TS) for haptic feedback with human participants in an underwater study in ocean and closed-water environments;

exploration of closed-water experiments of simulated wall-following to gauge the robot’s effectiveness at directing human participants;

demonstration of form, fit, and function of the current design in open-water field experiments for TS and maneuverability near coral reefs.

1. introduction of a novel, wearable underwater robotic system for movement assistance;

2. presentation of systematic testing of threshold sensitivity (TS) for haptic feedback with human participants in an underwater study in ocean and closed-water environments;

3. exploration of closed-water experiments of simulated wall-following to gauge the robot’s effectiveness at directing human participants;

4. demonstration of form, fit, and function of the current design in open-water field experiments for TS and maneuverability near coral reefs.

Construction remains one of the least automated sectors despite persistent labor shortages, hazardous working conditions, and declining productivity [ 1 ] . This stands in sharp contrast to more structured environments like manufacturing [ 2 ] and warehousing [ 3 ] , where advances in robotics and artificial intelligence have automated many core tasks. A key barrier to automating construction labor is the dynamic and unstructured nature of construction sites, requiring robots that navigate human-scale spaces and perform diverse tasks that combine mobility with dexterous manipulation [ 1 ] . Existing construction automation has largely focused on task-specific systems, either in controlled, off-site environments where robots perform repetitive tasks such as frame prefabrication [ 4 ] [ 5 ] , or directly on construction sites for activities such as bricklaying, drywall installation, and robotic assembly [ 5 ] [ 6 ] . This motivates the development of robotic systems that can operate directly in construction sites while supporting a broader range of tasks.

Humanoids are a natural fit for on-site construction work because their morphology matches spaces and tools built for humans. They can navigate tight spaces, climb steps, and use common tools without site modification or task-specific automation [ 1 ] . This allows humanoids to address a broad range of tasks with a single embodiment. While the existing humanoid models may not be optimal for every task, they offer reach and mobility no fixed or wheeled system can match [ 1 ] . However, reliable, full autonomy of humanoid robots remains out of the reach in the noise and clutter of a real construction site [ 1 ] .

Teleoperation offers a practical near-term path: skilled operators control robots remotely, reducing physical strain and hazard to workers while generating high-quality demonstration data for training future autonomous models. Manual material handling is a leading cause of musculoskeletal disorders [ 7 ] , and over 1,000 construction workers die on the job each year in the United States [ 8 ] . These conditions compound a persistent labor shortage, as an aging workforce approaches retirement while the physical toll deters new entrants [ 9 ] . By transferring strain and hazard to the robot while leveraging the operator’s expertise, teleoperation can improve worker safety and career longevity. Moreover, recent whole-body teleoperation systems have shown that expert demonstrations are key to training autonomous vision-language-action (VLA) models [ 10 , 11 , 12 ] . However, existing whole-body teleoperation systems have been evaluated primarily on household and laboratory tasks, leaving their effectiveness for construction work largely unexplored.

In this work, we develop a teleoperation system for a humanoid construction robot from a seated workstation using a Meta Quest 3 extended-reality (XR) headset for head and hand tracking and GLYDR foot pedals for locomotion control. We then deploy a Unitree G1 humanoid robot on an active construction site and perform a feasibility study on two high-importance construction tasks selected from the O*NET occupational database [ 13 ] . Our key contributions are:

A humanoid robot teleoperation system allowing seated, remote operation for simultaneous manipulation and locomotion built using open source components;

A humanoid robot teleoperation system allowing seated, remote operation for simultaneous manipulation and locomotion built using open source components;

Deployment on an active construction site;

A preliminary evaluation of teleoperation performance on two representative construction tasks.

1. A humanoid robot teleoperation system allowing seated, remote operation for simultaneous manipulation and locomotion built using open source components;

2. Deployment on an active construction site;

3. A preliminary evaluation of teleoperation performance on two representative construction tasks.

2026-10-05.introduction:
Robots plan and simulate on convex collision geometry. Sampling-based motion planners [ 1 , 2 ] , rigid-body simulators [ 3 , 4 , 5 , 6 ] , and contact-rich manipulation pipelines all rely on fast, robust proximity and penetration queries between convex bodies, computed with algorithms such as GJK and EPA [ 7 , 8 ] . Objects are therefore represented as convex decompositions , i.e., unions of convex parts that approximate a triangle mesh, and production simulators routinely build them with tools such as V-HACD [ 9 ] and CoACD [ 10 ] .

Image-to-3D generative models now offer a direct route from a camera image to 3D geometry. The strongest methods sample a high-quality shape latent with a large image-conditioned diffusion or flow-matching model in a single feed-forward pass [ 11 , 12 , 13 ] , and robotics increasingly uses them to build scene geometry for manipulation and planning [ 14 , 15 , 16 ] . However, these models are optimized for visual fidelity, not physical simulation . They output dense triangle meshes ( ∼ 10 6 {\sim}10^{6} faces) with small but near-ubiquitous topological defects such as non-manifold edges and stray fragments (quantified in § IV-A ). Turning such a mesh into collision geometry takes a fragile chain of decimation [ 17 ] , repair [ 18 ] , watertightening, and approximate convex decomposition. This reconstruct-then-decompose workflow [ 15 ] is slow and sensitive to errors at every stage. Skipping it is worse, because it does not fail loudly: as we show in § IV-B , physics engines accept the raw mesh and silently substitute a single convex hull, producing a plausible-looking scene with the wrong collision shape.

We propose I2CD ( I mage-to- C onvex- D ecomposition), which bypasses this workflow by predicting the convex decomposition directly from a single RGB image (Fig. 1 ). Our key insight is that the generator’s mesh is the wrong output but its features are the right input: a pretrained image-to-3D model already encodes the object’s shape, so only the readout needs to change. We therefore keep the image-conditioned diffusion transformer (DiT) and ShapeVAE decoder of Hunyuan3D-2 [ 11 ] frozen and replace the mesh readout with a lightweight cross-attention head. Its K K learned “convex-slot” tokens query the pretrained shape features, and each emits the H H halfplanes of one convex polytope in the CvxNet formulation [ 19 ] ; the union of the K K polytopes is the predicted shape. Only this head is trained, with a class-balanced occupancy loss and a convex-pair contrastive loss that suppresses the slab-stacking failure mode of CvxNet-style decoders.

I2CD introduces neither a new convex representation, which we inherit from CvxNet, nor a new 3D generator. Our contribution is to show, and validate for robotics, that a modern image-to-3D foundation model becomes a direct source of simulation geometry with a 38 M-parameter head and under ten GPU-hours of training, inheriting an open-world shape prior that, to our knowledge, no image-to-convex model trained from scratch currently offers. The single-view modes of CvxNet [ 19 ] and BSP-Net [ 20 ] are category-specific and have no open-world weights (§ II ). In summary, we:

adapt a frozen Hunyuan3D-2 backbone to emit K K halfplane-parameterized convexes directly, with a training objective that yields compact, spatially coherent parts;

benchmark against eight reconstruct-then-decompose pipelines (Hunyuan3D-2 and SAM3D [ 13 ] paired with CoACD, V-HACD, CvxNet, and BSP-Net) on 227 227 held-out OmniObject3D [ 21 ] and GSO [ 22 ] objects, where I2CD achieves the best volumetric IoU at 6 6 – 37 × 37\times lower end-to-end latency;

test simulation-readiness beyond “it loads” with a drop-and-settle study in MuJoCo, PyBullet, Genesis, and Isaac Sim (PhysX): raw generated meshes are silently replaced by a different shape or require costly preprocessing, while I2CD geometry is used as delivered in every engine;

execute 100 100 pick-and-place trials on a physical xArm7 in a 20 20 -object cluttered scene, where I2CD cuts the time to planner-ready scene geometry from 328 328 s to 11 11 s at comparable execution success ( 85 85 vs. 90 90 of 100 100 ).

1. adapt a frozen Hunyuan3D-2 backbone to emit K K halfplane-parameterized convexes directly, with a training objective that yields compact, spatially coherent parts;

2. benchmark against eight reconstruct-then-decompose pipelines (Hunyuan3D-2 and SAM3D [ 13 ] paired with CoACD, V-HACD, CvxNet, and BSP-Net) on 227 227 held-out OmniObject3D [ 21 ] and GSO [ 22 ] objects, where I2CD achieves the best volumetric IoU at 6 6 – 37 × 37\times lower end-to-end latency;

3. test simulation-readiness beyond “it loads” with a drop-and-settle study in MuJoCo, PyBullet, Genesis, and Isaac Sim (PhysX): raw generated meshes are silently replaced by a different shape or require costly preprocessing, while I2CD geometry is used as delivered in every engine;

4. execute 100 100 pick-and-place trials on a physical xArm7 in a 20 20 -object cluttered scene, where I2CD cuts the time to planner-ready scene geometry from 328 328 s to 11 11 s at comparable execution success ( 85 85 vs. 90 90 of 100 100 ).

2026-10-06.introduction:
Robotic grasping systems must remain reliable when object identity, pose, illumination, camera viewpoint, and hardware differ from the conditions represented during training. Although large public and synthetic datasets enable scalable end-to-end grasp learning, their visual statistics, sensor noise, robot embodiment, gripper geometry, and workspace configuration may not match a specific deployment. This domain gap can degrade grasp precision on the target system. Collecting a comparably large local dataset and retraining or fine-tuning a predictor for every installation is costly. The practical problem addressed in this work is therefore to achieve accurate grasping from only a small number of locally acquired and annotated examples.

RAGrasp addresses this problem through retrieval and adaptation, drawing inspiration from retrieval-augmented models that separate external memory from inference [ 1 ] . Rather than learning a task-specific grasp predictor from a public corpus, it builds a compact template bank from RGB-D observations collected with the deployment hardware. Given a new observation, the method isolates the query object, retrieves compatible local templates using geometric and semantic evidence, transfers the stored grasp through a confidence-gated dual-path estimator, and refines the result using mask-support and silhouette-contact constraints. A verified execution can optionally be stored as a new template. This design separates reusable grasp experience from the transfer procedure and expands deployment coverage without retraining the full system.

The main contributions are:

a data-efficient grasping formulation that uses a small, locally collected template bank instead of task-specific training on a large public grasp dataset;

a geometry-semantic retrieval cascade that combines scaled multi-start contour ICP with masked DINOv2 ranking;

a confidence-gated dual transfer mechanism that chooses between contour alignment and feature-correspondence-based similarity estimation;

a mask-constrained local refinement stage followed by calibrated metric execution and success-conditioned memory write-back; and

a real-world evaluation across seen and unseen objects, viewpoint and illumination changes, template-bank sizes, similarity strata, and component ablations.

1. a data-efficient grasping formulation that uses a small, locally collected template bank instead of task-specific training on a large public grasp dataset;

2. a geometry-semantic retrieval cascade that combines scaled multi-start contour ICP with masked DINOv2 ranking;

3. a confidence-gated dual transfer mechanism that chooses between contour alignment and feature-correspondence-based similarity estimation;

4. a mask-constrained local refinement stage followed by calibrated metric execution and success-conditioned memory write-back; and

5. a real-world evaluation across seen and unseen objects, viewpoint and illumination changes, template-bank sizes, similarity strata, and component ablations.

2026-10-07.introduction:
While research on large language models (LLMs) for human-robot interaction ( hri ) has dramatically increased since the release of ChatGPT in 2022, it has become well-recognized in the broad research community that using LLMs for robots, particularly spoken dialogue with robots, has some major hurdles [ 1 , 2 ] . Spoken dialogue systems ( sds ) offer a better approach for humans to interact with robots than off-the-shelf LLMs because sds research has shown effective ways to handle common-but-complex spoken interaction artifacts, such as misunderstandings, requests for clarification, turn-taking strategies, making decisions about what to say next, and interfacing with knowledge bases and external agents. Moreover, sds research has informed advancements in models for multimodal interaction, speech recognition, language understanding, dialogue management, dialogue state-tracking, as well as natural language generation, each of which are important aspects of spoken dialogue communication. Given this background and the current state of research and technology, the SDS+HRI community has also highlighted a need for a common benchmark that can drive research towards a shared goal of natural communication between people and robots [ 3 ] .

What does it take to have natural dialogue with robots? Past work identified five important technical requirements [ 4 ] ; specifically, a robot-ready sds must be:

modular : robot components are modular and individual modules must be able to integrate with sds modules;

multimodal : robots are situated dialogue partners whose many sensors must be integrated with sds speech input;

distributive : robot and sds modules should easily communicate with each other in a distributed environment;

incremental : modules must be able to process input quickly and immediately; and

aligned : sensors must be synchronized (aligned) in time.

Table I showcases some of the differences between robot-ready sds requirements and standard LLM capabilities. Though ongoing work makes LLMs more capable of spoken, granular interaction, these differences are important as humans expect robots to be capable of more natural communication, particularly if the robot has anthropomorphic features [ 5 ] .

In building towards a community that can build and evaluate robot-ready sds for use in HRI settings, we propose a benchmark and a framework, each detailed below.

1. modular : robot components are modular and individual modules must be able to integrate with sds modules;

2. multimodal : robots are situated dialogue partners whose many sensors must be integrated with sds speech input;

3. distributive : robot and sds modules should easily communicate with each other in a distributed environment;

4. incremental : modules must be able to process input quickly and immediately; and

5. aligned : sensors must be synchronized (aligned) in time.

Endovascular interventions are guided almost entirely by fluoroscopy. Clinicians advance a guidewire or catheter while viewing a continuous X-ray stream, running the imaging system throughout much of the procedure. The resulting radiation burden presents a recognized occupational hazard for interventional teams and a dose concern for patients; professional guidelines advise operators to keep exposure as low as reasonably achievable and treat every second of beam-on time as a cost [ 1 ] . Current dose-reduction practices center on pulsed acquisition, collimation, and operator discipline. The imaging schedule itself, however, remains essentially continuous because operators lack alternative sources of information regarding device location.

Robotics provides a candidate for that missing information source. A world model is an internal simulation of the environment that an agent rolls forward to predict action consequences, querying real observations only to correct internal drift [ 2 , 3 ] . The components for a physical catheter world model already exist across separate literatures. Cosserat rod theory provides a mechanically grounded model of slender flexible instruments under tendon actuation and external loading [ 4 ] , and has been applied to intraoperative contact-force estimation [ 5 ] . Open simulation frameworks support interactive rod and contact mechanics for medical scenarios [ 6 ] . Deep networks segment catheters in fluoroscopy frames in real time [ 7 ] , enabling acquired frames to serve as direct measurements. Cardiovascular digital-twin research emphasizes exactly this form of patient-specific predictive model [ 8 ] . In principle, procedures could rely primarily on internal simulation, querying X-rays sparsely at low frame rates or on demand to reset accumulated error.

What is missing is not forward prediction, but calibrated belief. Between observations, the internal model operates open-loop, with error accumulating from contact, friction, and actuation noise. A system providing an operator with a confident but inaccurate tip position, or an incorrect wall contact force, is worse than no assistance. The fundamental question for a sparse-imaging world model is therefore whether it recognizes when its estimate becomes unreliable: whether its reported uncertainty is statistically honest, and whether safety metrics derived from the belief, such as contact-force threshold exceedance, remain informative as observation frequency decreases.

This paper takes an initial, focused step toward addressing that question. Our contributions are:

a POMDP formulation of catheter navigation under sparse fluoroscopy, where the transition model is a Cosserat rod simulator, the observation model is a sparse noisy projection, and safety is evaluated as a contact-force exceedance probability under the belief;

a planar proof-of-concept implementation pairing a quasi-static rod simulator with a particle-filter belief over device state, reduced here to a tip state with a geometric contact proxy;

a calibration-focused evaluation protocol reporting tip tracking error (1.30 mm at one fifteenth of continuous frame rate), empirical coverage of reported credible intervals (0.92), and unsafe contact event discrimination (AUROC 0.68) across reduced observation rates.

We state the experimental scope directly. All evaluations run on a synthetic planar vessel phantom with a simplified quasi-static rod model; there are no clinical or animal data, no real fluoroscopic streams, and no human operator in the loop. These results do not constitute a claim of clinical readiness. Our goal is to establish the formulation and evaluation protocol at a focused scale where calibration can be assessed rigorously, providing a clear foundation for future work on complex anatomy and imaging.

1. a POMDP formulation of catheter navigation under sparse fluoroscopy, where the transition model is a Cosserat rod simulator, the observation model is a sparse noisy projection, and safety is evaluated as a contact-force exceedance probability under the belief;

2. a planar proof-of-concept implementation pairing a quasi-static rod simulator with a particle-filter belief over device state, reduced here to a tip state with a geometric contact proxy;

3. a calibration-focused evaluation protocol reporting tip tracking error (1.30 mm at one fifteenth of continuous frame rate), empirical coverage of reported credible intervals (0.92), and unsafe contact event discrimination (AUROC 0.68) across reduced observation rates.

2026-10-08.introduction:
Developing and evaluating robot policies through real-world trials is costly and difficult to scale. Simulation offers an alternative for policy evaluation and training ( Li et al., 2025 ; Ranawaka et al., 2026 ) . For a policy intended to return to a captured workspace, the simulated scene must support task-relevant interactions, and the policy must operate on observations available to the real robot.

Existing systems already bridge parts of this chain. Re 3 Sim reconstructs interactive scenes and demonstrates sim-to-real policy transfer ( Han et al., 2025 ) ; Agentic Real2Sim uses vision-language agents to build physics-based episodic scenes for downstream policy evaluation and fine-tuning ( Chen et al., 2026a ) ; and SimFoundry turns video into interactive scenes for simulation-based evaluation and sim-to-real policy training ( Ranawaka et al., 2026 ) . In programmatic control, Code as Policies generates executable programs over perception and control APIs ( Liang et al., 2023 ) , while CaP-X studies multi-turn execution feedback and transfers code-based policies using tools shared across simulation and reality ( Fu et al., 2026 ) . This motivates a more specific question: how can agent-refined reconstruction of an observed workspace be coupled with an execution-grounded programmatic policy and a consistent task-level interface across simulation and hardware?

We present Agentic Real-to-Sim-to-Real (Agentic RSR) to study this connection. Given a workspace video, a task, and a known robot, a Codex agent ( OpenAI, 2026 ) reconstructs a metrically aligned, interactive scene and refines it against visual and task-relevant physical checks. The same Codex agent ( OpenAI, 2026 ) then develops an executable programmatic policy, progressing from privileged object poses to visual observations and randomized simulation. The policy may be revised during development, but its version is locked during final evaluation. A shared task-level interface carries the policy and experience memory to hardware, where fresh observations and execution feedback support verification and recovery. Real-robot experiments show that the policies retain much of their aggregate task performance after transfer from simulation.

Our contributions are:

Agentic Real-to-Sim scene reconstruction for executable tasks. We anchor the metric scale of a video-reconstructed workspace to known robot geometry, iteratively reconstruct the scene using reference views and depth and appearance feedback, and check task-relevant geometric and physical interactions so that the scene supports robot execution;

An execution-grounded programmatic policy developed by a Codex agent. The Codex agent ( OpenAI, 2026 ) generates a policy bundle containing a complete program and reusable steps. The bundle remains locked during an episode, while the Codex agent ( OpenAI, 2026 ) uses execution results and fresh observations to decide whether to continue, switch steps, retry, or recover;

A unified Agentic Real-to-Sim-to-Real closed-loop framework. A consistent task-level observation and action interface connects scene reconstruction, simulation-based policy testing, and hardware execution, where execution feedback supports verification and recovery.

1. Agentic Real-to-Sim scene reconstruction for executable tasks. We anchor the metric scale of a video-reconstructed workspace to known robot geometry, iteratively reconstruct the scene using reference views and depth and appearance feedback, and check task-relevant geometric and physical interactions so that the scene supports robot execution;

2. An execution-grounded programmatic policy developed by a Codex agent. The Codex agent ( OpenAI, 2026 ) generates a policy bundle containing a complete program and reusable steps. The bundle remains locked during an episode, while the Codex agent ( OpenAI, 2026 ) uses execution results and fresh observations to decide whether to continue, switch steps, retry, or recover;

3. A unified Agentic Real-to-Sim-to-Real closed-loop framework. A consistent task-level observation and action interface connects scene reconstruction, simulation-based policy testing, and hardware execution, where execution feedback supports verification and recovery.

Robotic manipulation of fragile objects requires a robot to recognize two very different kinds of failure: the object slipping out of the gripper, and the object fracturing inside it. Slip has received considerable attention, since it can be inferred from force, vibration, or visual cues and corrected before the object is completely dropped. Fracture is a harder problem and a comparatively underexplored one. A sensing and control system that only handles slip, or only handles fracture, addresses half the problem. Fully characterizing the safe grasping range requires estimating both the lower boundary (slip) and the upper boundary (fracture). Relying on slip feedback alone creates a trade-off between speed and safety: reaching a stable grip quickly requires larger force increments, which raises the risk of fracturing the object before slip is even detected.

Detection means something different for each failure mode. For slip, there is typically a reaction window between the onset of relative motion and the object fully leaving the gripper, giving a controller time to increase force before the grasp is lost. For fracture, detection instead marks a state in which a crack is already present. What detection provides here is not prevention of that first instant of damage, but the ability to halt further force escalation and contain it before it propagates, rather than continuing to apply an escalating grasp force under the false assumption that the object remains intact. This distinction matters more as robots move into complicated environments, such as household settings, where the same robot needs to execute a sequence of real-time-decided manipulations. Relying purely on pre-tuned, fixed-force policies becomes increasingly limiting in this setting. If the first step in a sequence induces slip, the next step must adjust its grasp accordingly, whereas if the first step fractures the object, whether to continue the original plan at all becomes a decision the robot must be able to make, rather than one it can silently ignore.

Existing approaches to this problem generally fall into two lines of work, which we review in detail in Section II . Directly instrumenting the gripper, whether with multimodal sensors [ 23 ] , reactive tactile force control [ 1 ] , or pneumatic and barometric channels [ 22 ] , has produced effective slip-regulating controllers, but comparatively little work treats fracture as a distinct, detectable failure mode rather than an incidental force spike [ 17 , 7 , 28 ] , and such hardware is often tailored to a specific gripper design. On the acoustic sensing side, high-frequency vibrations are directly emitted during fracture initiation and can be used for real-time monitoring [ 3 ] . Existing PVDF-based acoustic sensing methods have mainly focused on detecting slip-induced vibrations using handcrafted signal processing and threshold-based decision rules [ 16 , 21 ] . Whether the same sensing modality can also distinguish fracture events, and whether a unified learned model can reliably recognize both slip and fracture for real-time closed-loop control, remains unexplored.

We present SAFE ( S lip A nd F racture d E tection), a framework that answers this question with a deliberately minimal sensing layer containing two passive PVDF acoustic films mounted on opposite surfaces of a Fin Ray finger, combined with the motor’s own current and position feedback. Unlike prior acoustic slip detectors, SAFE is trained as a unified three-class classifier that distinguishes slip, fracture, and normal events from one 79-dimensional feature vector, running at approximately 104 Hz on a single NVIDIA Jetson Orin Nano. Because the sensing hardware is passive, low-cost, and adds no meaningful stiffness to the finger, and the classifier requires no additional compute or actuation hardware, SAFE itself can provide sufficient data for a closed-loop adaptive grasp controller and can also serve as an add-on providing additional grasping state data to other grasping platforms. Evaluated under leave-one-grasp-out cross-validation, SAFE achieves an Alert-F1 of 0.884 with near-zero confusion between slip and fracture, and the resulting adaptive controller reaches a 91.3% closed-loop grasp success rate across 46 real-robot trials, compared to 71.7% and 60.9% for fixed-force baselines. It further reaches 82.4% success across 51 trials on object categories entirely absent from training, showing that the same sensing layer generalizes beyond the specific objects it was trained on.

The contributions of this work are as follows:

A low-cost, two-channel passive acoustic sensing scheme, mounted on a Fin Ray finger, shown through ablation to be required for reliable slip and fracture detection;

A unified three-class classifier that distinguishes slip, fracture, and normal contact with near-zero cross-confusion (3 of 437 alert windows assigned to the opposite failure mode), evaluated under a leave-one-grasp-out protocol that avoids trial-level leakage;

An adaptive grasp controller that integrates this detection layer in closed loop, achieving 91.3% grasp success on five object categories, compared to 71.7% and 60.9% for fixed-force baselines, and 82.4% success across 51 trials on three object categories entirely absent from the training data.

1. A low-cost, two-channel passive acoustic sensing scheme, mounted on a Fin Ray finger, shown through ablation to be required for reliable slip and fracture detection;

2. A unified three-class classifier that distinguishes slip, fracture, and normal contact with near-zero cross-confusion (3 of 437 alert windows assigned to the opposite failure mode), evaluated under a leave-one-grasp-out protocol that avoids trial-level leakage;

3. An adaptive grasp controller that integrates this detection layer in closed loop, achieving 91.3% grasp success on five object categories, compared to 71.7% and 60.9% for fixed-force baselines, and 82.4% success across 51 trials on three object categories entirely absent from the training data.

Understanding how humans grasp objects is a fundamental problem at the intersection of embodied intelligence, computer vision, and computer graphics. Accurate reconstruction of hand-object grasps from images is needed for many applications including digital human modeling, AR/VR, robot learning from human demonstrations, and general understanding of dexterous manipulation.

Existing hand-object reconstruction methods have achieved impressive progress in recovering visually plausible grasps from images. Both optimization-based approaches ( Liu et al., 2024 ) and feed-forward models ( Chen et al., 2025 ; Pavlakos et al., 2024 ; Potamias et al., 2025 ; Wang et al., 2025 ) can now produce high-quality 3D hand poses with reasonable hand-object spatial relationships that are consistent with the input image. However, most of these methods stop at visual fidelity: the poses are evaluated in terms of reconstruction accuracy, pose consistency, and/or geometric plausibility (i.e. the hand is in contact with, but does not penetrate, the object). A grasp may look convincing when rendered in 3D while still being impossible to execute or physically unstable in reality.

More recent work (surveyed below in Section 2 ) recognizes the importance of physics in grasp reconstruction and synthesis. A common approach is to analyze stability of the grasped object in terms of force closure, which characterizes whether frictional contact forces at a static set of contact points could, in principle, hold the object in place against gravity. Such analysis does not address how the hand should actually be posed and actuated, and the corresponding grasp may still be impossible if the hand cannot produce and maintain the required contact forces.

We argue that stable grasp reconstruction is fundamentally a control problem, and cannot be treated as a visual or geometric optimization task divorced from inference of joint torques that hold the object stably in place over time. For example, a hand may appear to pinch a cup, yet if the pinch force is insufficient or unevenly distributed, the cup will slip or fall rather than remain firmly put. We therefore present StableGrasp, a method for monocular stable human grasp reconstruction that evaluates stability from the viewpoint of the coupled dynamics of the hand and object under simulation. StableGrasp also achieves strong visual stability: the reconstructed pose closely matches the image observations, and during simulation, the reconstructed grasp remains nearly motionless.
To the best of our knowledge, StableGrasp is the first method to pursue this combination of rigorous physical stability and visual fidelity.

We evaluate our method on several established datasets ( DexYCB ( Chao et al., 2021 ) , HO3D ( Hampali et al., 2020 ) , and CORe50 ( Lomonaco and Maltoni, 2017 ) ) against baselines such as heuristic ( Zhong et al., 2025 ) and learning-based ( Christen et al., 2022 ) hand control. Our results show that compared to these baseline methods, StableGrasp substantially improves physical stability while producing reconstructed grasps that are visually consistent with the images and geometrically plausible.

In summary:

we formulate monocular stable human grasp reconstruction with a notion of physical stability defined by forward simulation of the full hand-object system;

we propose a two-phase physics-based refinement framework that introduces a kinetic-energy-driven optimization for stable grasp reconstruction, while preserving the visual and geometric fidelity of the reconstructed grasp;

we use this framework to build a complete monocular reconstruction pipeline that recovers both the visual grasp configuration and a control target whose induced joint torques stabilize the grasp in physical simulation.

1. we formulate monocular stable human grasp reconstruction with a notion of physical stability defined by forward simulation of the full hand-object system;

2. we propose a two-phase physics-based refinement framework that introduces a kinetic-energy-driven optimization for stable grasp reconstruction, while preserving the visual and geometric fidelity of the reconstructed grasp;

3. we use this framework to build a complete monocular reconstruction pipeline that recovers both the visual grasp configuration and a control target whose induced joint torques stabilize the grasp in physical simulation.

