A Markov Decision Process (MDP) is defined by ℳ = ( 𝒮 , 𝒜 , r , ρ 0 , P , γ ) \mathcal{M}=(\mathcal{S},\mathcal{A},r,\rho_{0},P,\gamma) , with state space 𝒮 \mathcal{S} , action space 𝒜 \mathcal{A} , reward function r ⁡ ( s , a ) : 𝒮 × 𝒜 → ℝ r({\color[rgb]{0.5352,0.5352,0.5352}s},{\color[rgb]{0.5352,0.5352,0.5352}a}):\mathcal{S}\times\mathcal{A}{}\to\mathbb{R} , initial state distribution ρ 0 ∈ Δ ⁡ ( 𝒮 ) \rho_{0}\in\Delta(\mathcal{S}) , transition dynamics function P ⁡ ( s ′ | s , a ) : 𝒮 × 𝒜 → Δ ⁡ ( 𝒮 ) P({\color[rgb]{0.5352,0.5352,0.5352}s^{\prime}}|{\color[rgb]{0.5352,0.5352,0.5352}s},{\color[rgb]{0.5352,0.5352,0.5352}a}):\mathcal{S}\times\mathcal{A}\to\Delta(\mathcal{S}) , and discount factor γ ∈ [ 0 , 1 ] \gamma\in[0,1] . Given a dataset of trajectories 𝒟 \mathcal{D} generated by some behavior policy π β ​ ( a | s ) : 𝒮 → Δ ⁡ ( 𝒜 ) \pi_{\beta}({\color[rgb]{0.5352,0.5352,0.5352}a}|{\color[rgb]{0.5352,0.5352,0.5352}s}):\mathcal{S}\to\Delta(\mathcal{A}) , offline RL aims to learn a policy π A ​ ( a | s ) : 𝒮 → Δ ⁡ ( 𝒜 ) \pi_{\textsf{A}}{}({\color[rgb]{0.5352,0.5352,0.5352}a}|{\color[rgb]{0.5352,0.5352,0.5352}s}):\mathcal{S}\to\Delta(\mathcal{A}) that maximizes the expected discounted return R ⁡ ( π A ) = 𝔼 τ ∼ ρ π A ​ ( τ ) ​ [ ∑ h = 0 H γ h ​ r ​ ( s h , a h ) ] R(\pi_{\textsf{A}}{})=\mathbb{E}_{\tau\sim\rho^{\pi_{\textsf{A}}{}}({\color[rgb]{0.5352,0.5352,0.5352}\tau})}\left[\sum_{h=0}^{H}\gamma^{h}r(s_{h},a_{h})\right] , where ρ π A ( τ ) = ρ 0 ( s 0 ) π A ( a 0 | s 0 ) P ( s 1 | s 0 , a 0 ) ⋯ π A ( a H | s H ) \rho^{\pi_{\textsf{A}}{}}(\tau)=\rho_{0}(s_{0})\pi_{\textsf{A}}{}(a_{0}|s_{0})P(s_{1}|s_{0},a_{0})\cdots\pi_{\textsf{A}}{}(a_{H}|s_{H}) . Note that sampling trajectories during training is not allowed in the offline RL setting.

