2026-10-15.introduction:
Volumetric occupancy mapping is a core building block of autonomous navigation, and its real-time cost is dominated by the finest resolution at which measurements are integrated and stored [ 5 , 8 , 10 ] .
A system that uses centimeter-scale fidelity everywhere also incurs it’s cost everywhere, even though most of a large environment is never on, or even close to, the area the robot will actually navigate on.

This paper turns that observation into a mechanism: allocate resolution by plan-relevance, finely mapping near the plan and coarsely everywhere else, as shown in Fig. 1 , according to the diagram presented in Fig. 2 .
The natural objection – why not map everything coarse? – is exactly the discriminator our experiments are built around: a uniform coarse map quantizes the terrain the robot must traverse, degrading and over-optimizing the paths the planner reasons about.
Tiering is only worth defending if it avoids that failure while approaching the coarse map’s savings, and on the harder of our two real-world scenarios, that is precisely the trade it achieves.

This paper contributes:

An online, plan-conditioned resolution-allocation mechanism and a resolution router that split each LiDAR sweep into a fine and a coarse stream by distance from the robot center or the live planned-path corridor and integrates both into a single hashed-wavelet octree via two simultaneous integrators;

An online, plan-conditioned resolution-allocation mechanism and a resolution router that split each LiDAR sweep into a fine and a coarse stream by distance from the robot center or the live planned-path corridor and integrates both into a single hashed-wavelet octree via two simultaneous integrators;

An evaluation methodology isolating resolution allocation as the sole independent variable across six configurations, scored offline under a shared, independent reference map;

A secondary ablation against a plan-free, budget-matched sensor-range baseline.

Section II situates this against prior mapping work; Section III describes the mechanism; Section IV presents the evaluation’s results and discussion, and Section V presents the conclusion and future work.

1. An online, plan-conditioned resolution-allocation mechanism and a resolution router that split each LiDAR sweep into a fine and a coarse stream by distance from the robot center or the live planned-path corridor and integrates both into a single hashed-wavelet octree via two simultaneous integrators;

2. An evaluation methodology isolating resolution allocation as the sole independent variable across six configurations, scored offline under a shared, independent reference map;

3. A secondary ablation against a plan-free, budget-matched sensor-range baseline.

Going back to work after having recovered from an upper-limb injury is a delicate moment for workers, because they might have not completely retrieved their full mobility prior to the accident. Collaborative robots and humanoids are progressively appearing in industrial environments and could facilitate this process by adapting their behaviour to the physical capabilities of individual workers [ 1 , 2 ] . Such adaptations require robots to estimate the user’s residual functional mobility.
With such knowledge, robots can further tune the physical interaction with the user to make their reintegration in the workplace more manageable and personalised.

Additionally, robots have the potential to plan their own motions and collaborate with workers to avoid compensatory movements and take over the workers’ more proper motions related to work activity, because their functional mobility cannot yet be fully reengaged [ 3 , 4 ] . Although robotic solutions that can account for compensatory movements have been developed in rehabilitation settings [ 5 ] , a system capable of achieving the same purpose in industrial settings has yet to be designed. Indeed, the few existing works addressing upper-limb impairments in human-robot collaboration primarily substitute the user’s movements with robotic assistance or minimise the need for arm motion altogether [ 6 , 7 , 8 ] , rather than explicitly modelling and adapting to the user-specific joint level limitations.

In order to adapt an interaction to a worker’s physical capabilities, the robot requires a quantitative representation of the user’s mobility at the joint level. A joint’s range of motion (ROM) describes its overall admissible angular interval. However, the
portion of that interval available or expressed during a goal-directed activity may be smaller. We refer to this task-available interval as the functional range of motion (fROM). In clinical settings, scores like the Fugl-Meyer’s [ 9 ] are used to give a qualitative evaluation of the user’s ROMs. However, when it comes to human-aware motion planning, clinical scores lack in providing quantitative linking to the mobility at the joint level [ 10 ] , which could be used by a robot to properly tune the interaction according to the specific joint movements to elicit [ 11 ] . Furthermore, clinical scores are measured while having patients performing unnatural movements, which could alter the functional ROM (fROM) during more usual work activity. In relation to the upper limb, previous attempts of measuring its ROMs have already been performed in literature. Keyvanian et al. [ 12 ] explored an unsupervised learning approach to learn ROM boundary functions, by collecting full-range movements of healthy people, exploring one by one all the possible ROMs of the upper arm. They then defined an impairment index, a high-level quantitative metrics of the user’s impairment that takes into account all the ROMs estimated altogether. However, in this approach, they explored free movements of the arm without focusing on goal-oriented and manipulation tasks [ 13 ] , having participants extensively move each joint at a time to their fullest extent. Considering the upper limb, the engagement of each degree of freedom (DOF) of it changes from using them one at a time and altogether within a goal-oriented action [ 14 ] . Therefore, the fROMs of the user acting in a setting may differ. Because of this, Liu et al. [ 15 ] proposed a framework that leveraged information from task-based recordings of people with different ROMs. Upon receiving clinical assessment scores of the ROMs related to the recordings [ 9 ] , they learned a mapping from the clinical scores to the ROMs, so that the robot could learn the fROMs of a new user by using their clinical scores and without motion tracking. Although effective, this methodology relies on having the clinical scores available and updated. While these could be available at the beginning of the reintegration in the workplace, it can be demanding to summon a clinician again in the future to track and monitor the progress of the recovery outside of the clinical settings. A robot should be able to evaluate the ROMs of the user independently, without having to rely on external human inputs for this information.

Therefore, in this work, we developed a pipeline through which a collaborative robot estimates the impairment coefficients associated with the user’s upper-limb fROM through an active perception process (see Fig. 1 ). In this, the robotic system repetitively hands over an object to the human user. Based on the user’s kinematics at each handover pose, the belief over the impairment coefficients is updated. The resulting coefficients parameterise the modeled fROM bounds of the considered joints. This process is repeated until the system finds an accurate estimate of the fROMs inspected. No previous learning is requested to perform this estimation. We demonstrated the validity of this approach through an experimental validation in which a participant performed the requested handovers while having their upper arm movements artificially hindered. The contributions of this work are threefold:

a robot-centric formulation of functional upper-limb mobility through impairment coefficients;

a robot-centric formulation of functional upper-limb mobility through impairment coefficients;

an online active perception pipeline where the robot initiates handovers and updates a belief over the user’s functional mobility;

validation of the proposed framework through deployment on a real robotic platform interacting with a participant with enacted impairments.

This paper is structured as follows. Upon having introduced our work (see Section I ), Section II describes the human modeling adopted for the problem and the active perception pipeline used for the ROM perception problem. Section III and IV report the experiments carried out and the related results, respectively. Finally, Section V and VI reason about the interpretations of the results and summarise the content of the paper.

1. a robot-centric formulation of functional upper-limb mobility through impairment coefficients;

2. an online active perception pipeline where the robot initiates handovers and updates a belief over the user’s functional mobility;

3. validation of the proposed framework through deployment on a real robotic platform interacting with a participant with enacted impairments.

2026-10-15.abstract:
Integrating world models into Vision-Language-Action models (VLAs) has emerged as a promising direction, enabling policies to anticipate future dynamics beyond reactive observation-to-action mapping.
However, existing approaches vary simultaneously in their world representations, integration paradigms, and policy backbones, making it difficult to determine what truly matters for effective world model integration.
To disentangle these factors, we categorize existing efforts into four representative paradigms, namely Implicit Future Alignment, Explicit Future Query, MoT Future Expert, and External World Predictor.
We instantiate each within a unified framework and systematically evaluate these paradigms with diverse world representations and policy backbones across in-domain, out-of-domain, long-horizon, dynamic, few-shot, and real-world settings.
Our study yields four main findings:
1) world representation effectiveness is paradigm-dependent, whereas 2D semantic representations offer robust, cross-paradigm gains;
2) no single paradigm is universally optimal, yet the decoupled MoT Future Expert offers competitive overall performance, particularly in OOD robustness;
3) world modeling consistently benefits policies built on standard VLMs, while decoupled designs exhibit greater compatibility with pretrained VLAs;
and 4) world modeling improves performance over action-only baselines across multiple settings, with notable gains on long-horizon and dynamic tasks.
Project page and video demonstrations are available at https://dylanmqs.github.io/WM-in-VLAs/ .

2026-10-16.introduction:
Foundation models such as Vision-Language-Action Models (VLAs) [ 1 ] show incredible promise for robot control but require vast amounts of robot data, which is not easily obtainable. Teleoperation is a simple strategy for collecting robot data in the form of demonstrations but incurs a large human cost per task,
making it difficult to scale. To reduce the amount of human effort, recent work developed semi-automated demonstration generation systems that adapt a handful of human-teleoperated demonstrations to new initial states [ 2 ] .
However, these approaches still require annotated human demonstrations per task, preventing them from scaling autonomously to new tasks, and are typically deployed only in simulation because they produce unsafe behaviors.

Task and Motion Planning (TAMP) [ 3 ] systems are able to autonomously solve new tasks using the same planning model.
Prior work has used TAMP to generate demonstrations for imitation learning [ 4 , 5 ] , but due to the stochastic nature of prior TAMP algorithms, the resulting demonstrations have been difficult to learn from due to their suboptimality and multi-modality.
Recent work on TAMP leverages hardware acceleration, for example, on Graphics Processing Units (GPUs), to more efficiently solve problems [ 6 , 7 , 8 ] .
We directly investigate the impact of GPU acceleration not only on producing demonstrations quickly but also on optimizing them so that they are easier to learn from.

Our approach, DemoStream, first makes novel TAMP contributions in the form of batched streams , conditional samplers that generate constraint-satisfying outputs for a batch of input values, and batched computation graph optimization algorithms that compose multiple batched streams and cost functions in order to explore thousands of TAMP solutions in parallel.
These representation and algorithmic innovations give us the flexibility to explore multiple TAMP cost functions and analyze their effect on demonstration success rate, demonstration length, and downstream policy success rate.
Notably, we find that without solution optimization, TAMP systems still have high demonstration generation success rates but train policies with success rates of zero (Table I ).
Additionally, the choice of cost function matters; we find that it is easier to learn from demonstrations that minimize task-space rather than joint-space costs, both during TAMP and motion planning, likely because these costs minimize the motion of wrist-mounted cameras, shrinking the distribution of encountered observations.
DemoStream is able to train successful visuomotor policies on a variety of tasks including multi-robot (Figure 5 ), long-horizon (Figure 6 ), agentic open-world (Figure 7 ), and real-world (Figure 1 ) tasks.

The contributions of this paper are:

New TAMP batched samplers and computation graph optimizers for GPU-accelerated plan optimization;

Automated demonstration generation via agentic, batched TAMP for end-to-end imitation learning;

Analysis on the effect of action space, cost function, and batch size on policy learning success rates;

Simulation experiments demonstrating long-horizon, multi-robot, and open-world DemoStream capabilities;

Safe automated real-world demonstration collection.

1. New TAMP batched samplers and computation graph optimizers for GPU-accelerated plan optimization;

2. Automated demonstration generation via agentic, batched TAMP for end-to-end imitation learning;

3. Analysis on the effect of action space, cost function, and batch size on policy learning success rates;

4. Simulation experiments demonstrating long-horizon, multi-robot, and open-world DemoStream capabilities;

5. Safe automated real-world demonstration collection.

The collection of spatiotemporally varying data is critical for environmental and engineering applications, from characterizing ocean currents for climate modeling [ 1 , 2 ] to mapping wind speeds for offshore wind farm siting [ 3 , 4 ] . Because these tasks require spatially expansive and temporally persistent measurements, sustainably powered mobile robots—such as solar-powered autonomous surface vessels (ASVs) [ 5 ] and wind-driven sailing drones [ 6 ] —have become increasingly attractive.

Unlike traditional finite-horizon missions, persistently powered robots must continuously balance energy inflow (harvesting) with energy outflow (propulsion and sensing). Similarly, a central premise behind persistent missions is the presence of information gain resulting from observations and information decay resulting from locations going unvisited over time. We view this interaction through a coupled “reservoir” lens (Fig. 1 ). Harvested energy fills a physical battery reservoir, and this energy is converted into information, which possesses its own virtual reservoir. Because environmental information continuously decays due to forecast drift or measurement staleness [ 7 ] , long-term monitoring requires the robot to repeatedly revisit and refresh informative regions.

While a large body of literature addresses informative path planning [ 8 , 9 ] and energy-aware routing [ 10 , 11 ] , these frameworks often model missions as resource-constrained traveling salesperson problems [ 12 ] . Such routing formulations treat sensing as a discrete event collected at isolated nodes. Conversely, continuous approaches like ergodic search optimize trajectories over time-varying information maps [ 13 ] , but typically rely on finite-horizon optimization formulations. However, for a mobile sensor accruing continuous rewards, a discrete objective is insufficient, since a measurement taken at any point along a path affects not only the evolution of information at that point on the path but also the evolution of information at every other location.

To explicitly capture these dynamics, we leverage the quantity of clarity [ 14 ] , which describes the state of certainty about a particular point at a particular time and evolves according to an ODE containing both information gain and decay terms. Coupled with a standard nonlinear propulsion model (e.g., drag power proportional to speed cubed [ 15 ] ), the fundamental control problem shifts from discrete routing to computing a continuous speed trajectory that maximizes a reward functional that characterizes information over the entire path at each point in time.

Specifically, we consider a mobile robot that is tasked with optimizing its speed profile over a prescribed path, given limited available power, (generally) unequal importance allocated to certain portions of the path relative to others, and periodicity constraints on final vs. initial energy (robot state of charge) and clarity (information) states. The theoretical results center around the derivation and interpretation of necessary conditions for optimality under this problem formulation. After deriving these conditions, we present several properties associated with the nature of the optimal control strategy, effect of unequal importance weighting along the mission path, and limiting performance with respect to both the importance weighting and available power.

The theoretical results in this work are complemented by two simulation studies. The first study represents an illustrative “toy” example that shows how the robot’s speed profile varies according to the importance of information at different points along the path. The second study focuses on using a solar-powered autonomous surface vessel (ASV) to characterize salinity information in the Chesapeake Bay. Results of the simulation study illustrate the value of modifying the ASV’s speed profile by solving the optimization problem presented in this work.

In summary, the contributions of this paper are as follows:

A modeling framework coupling clarity dynamics with nonlinear energy expenditure;

An optimal control formulation for periodic sensing missions derived via Pontryagin’s Maximum Principle, yielding necessary conditions for optimality;

Analytical results establishing several properties of the optimal control solution;

Numerical validation via IPOPT, demonstrating optimal speed profiles that slow in high-information regions and accelerate through low-value regions;

A simulation case study for salinity monitoring in the Chesapeake Bay, showcasing the utility of the work in a realistic application domain.

2026-10-20.introduction:
Dexterous grasping in cluttered environments is essential for practical robotic manipulation. However, it remains challenging due to the high-dimensional action space and complex interactions among object, hand and surrounding environments. Existing methods typically generate multiple grasp proposals directly in the scene and rely on collision checking or grasp scoring to select executable grasps [ 37 , 39 , 16 , 9 , 19 , 7 , 38 ] . However, previous works have three key limitations. First, the proposal-and-selection paradigm suffers from low inference sample efficiency. Since the relations among the hand, object, and surrounding environment are not sufficiently encoded, many generated proposals are either in collision or dynamically unstable, requiring a large number of candidates to obtain a single successful grasp. Second, model performance is limited by insufficient data scale and diversity. Existing datasets are limited in object and embodiment diversity, with redundant annotations concentrated on a small set of objects [ 37 , 39 ] . Third, existing grasp representations are insufficient. Prior methods either rely on hand-specific representations, such as hand joint values [ 37 , 16 ] or predefined grasp templates [ 9 , 38 ] , which limit generalization across hands, or adopt object-centric representations for grasp optimization [ 39 , 11 ] , which is computationally expensive.

To address these limitations, we first construct a large-scale dataset across multiple embodiments for dexterous grasping in cluttered environments. Our benchmark contains around 0.5B grasp annotations in common manipulation scenes, with hierarchical difficulty scores that enable fine-grained evaluation across varying grasp difficulties. In addition, we propose 𝒯 ⁡ ( R , O ) \mathcal{T}(R,O) Graph++, a unified grasp representation that models spatial transformations among environment, robot, and object. Built upon 𝒯 ⁡ ( R , O ) \mathcal{T}(R,O) Graph++, our 𝒯 ⁡ ( R , O ) \mathcal{T}(R,O) Grasp++ model performs unified diffusion-based cross-embodiment grasping synthesis. In extensive experiments, our model achieves an average success rate of 87.96% across eight dexterous hands, an inference speed of 0.34 seconds on an NVIDIA 4090 GPU, and over 10 × \times higher inference sample efficiency than existing baselines.

In conclusion, our contributions are summarized as follows:
(1) We construct a large-scale cluttered-scene dexterous grasping benchmark with 53,675 objects, around 0.5B grasp annotations, and difficulty scores for evaluation under varying grasp difficulties;
(2) We propose 𝒯 ⁡ ( R , O ) \mathcal{T}(R,O) Grasp++, a scalable diffusion-based framework that integrates object-environment geometry with hand kinematics for cross-embodiment grasp synthesis in cluttered environments;
(3) We conduct extensive simulation and real-world experiments, where 𝒯 ⁡ ( R , O ) \mathcal{T}(R,O) Grasp++ significantly outperforms all baselines by over 30%, while achieving fast and sample-efficient inference;
(4) We present the first scaling study for cross-embodiment dexterous grasping, leveraging our large-scale benchmark to reveal how performance scales with training object diversity and model capacity.

Legged robots often operate over terrain that changes within a few steps, from flat ground to curbs, stairs, rubble, or uneven outdoor surfaces. Quadrupeds handle this variability by switching among locomotion gaits with different trade-offs in speed, stability, and clearance. Trotting is efficient on smooth ground but can become risky on rough terrain due to foot scuffing and body impacts. Climbing or stair gaits improve clearance and stability but are slower and inefficient when used unnecessarily.

A central challenge is not only how robots should walk, but when their manner of walking should change. Existing systems either rely on rich perception and learned policies for terrain-aware locomotion [ 1 ] , or use perception-driven heuristics that trigger gait switching from local terrain cues [ 2 ] . Here, reactive switching refers to gait switching decisions driven by instantaneous local cues rather than look-ahead, cost-aware planning. On physical platforms, reactive switching can be unreliable when gait transitions are disruptive or delayed by onboard controller logic and safety checks [ 3 ] .

This issue is pronounced on the Unitree Go2 studied in this work, where gait switching is asymmetric. Switching from climb to trot is essentially seamless. In contrast, switching from trot to climb requires the robot to stop and wait for the gait to engage. If it does not, the transition can be poorly handled and lead to stumbling. In some cases, the command is ignored and the robot continues trotting.
Simple reactive threshold rules can enforce conservative stop-and-wait behavior, but they do not decide when it is worth paying the transition cost based on what lies ahead. Height maps from low-cost LiDAR can also be noisy and can overestimate obstacles in vegetation such as tall grass, which can trigger unnecessary switching to climb gait.

To address this, we propose a lightweight gait switching supervisor for LiDAR-enabled quadrupeds that uses an instantaneous robot-centered height map and makes gait switching decisions using a Markov Decision Process (MDP). Fig. 1 provides an overview of the proposed pipeline. The forward height map is partitioned into distance bands that provide a compact look-ahead representation. Each band is summarized into a discrete terrain class using robust elevation statistics, and uncertainty in the band evolution is captured in the transition model. The resulting MDP explicitly accounts for asymmetric switching latency and produces an offline-computed policy for online gait switching.

The main contributions of this work are:

A height map only, anticipative gait switching supervisor that requires minimal sensing and is compatible with existing gait controllers;

An MDP formulation that models asymmetric gait transition latency, including a dwell state for trot-to-climb switching, and yields a compact optimal switching policy solved offline;

An empirical indoor and outdoor evaluation on the Unitree Go2 comparing the proposed look-ahead MDP policy against a tuned hysteresis baseline, demonstrating improved switching behavior under real transition constraints.

1. A height map only, anticipative gait switching supervisor that requires minimal sensing and is compatible with existing gait controllers;

2. An MDP formulation that models asymmetric gait transition latency, including a dwell state for trot-to-climb switching, and yields a compact optimal switching policy solved offline;

3. An empirical indoor and outdoor evaluation on the Unitree Go2 comparing the proposed look-ahead MDP policy against a tuned hysteresis baseline, demonstrating improved switching behavior under real transition constraints.

2026-10-23.introduction:
Lung Carcinoma, commonly known as lung cancer, is the leading cause of cancer-related death worldwide, accounting for 1.8 million deaths per year. Yet, biopsy for diagnosis still faces the challenge of hitting a moving target (a tumor within the lung tissue) and relies on a percutaneous CT-guided needle operated almost entirely by human hands with minimal robotic assistance [ 1 , 2 ] .
In clinical practice, the standard protocol for percutaneous CT-guided needle biopsy involves the clinician walking in and out of the operating room for each needle adjustment, leading to longer procedure times and increased scan radiation exposure for the patient and possibly the clinician or technician.

To address positional errors caused by the patient’s natural breathing cycle, physicians have implemented a breath-holding protocol in which the patient holds a stable lung position while the interventional radiologist inserts the needle toward the target. This protocol, while widely adopted, is often unsuccessful for those most likely to need this procedure: older patients with severe pulmonary diseases that make breath-holding difficult [ 1 , 2 , 3 , 4 ] .

Several sensing methods have been considered to address tumor tracking during breath cycles. Contact-based devices such as respiratory belts and bellows provide consistent respiratory signals by tracking changes in chest and abdominal expansion [ 3 , 5 ] , but they can cause patient discomfort. Optical markers attached to patients can track 3D chest motions through spatially resolved measurements, although they require external cameras and continuous marker visibility [ 6 ] . Contactless approaches such as structured light and time-of-flight cameras can also be used to measure chest surface motion without markers, though their effectiveness is influenced by factors including camera positioning, surface visibility, and the physical constraints around the CT scanner [ 7 , 8 , 9 ] .

Current robotics solutions have made strides in assisting with tumor localization and remove the need for a breath-holding protocol.
A robotic system for trans-thoracic puncture of pulmonary nodules based on gated respiratory compensation was developed by Li et al. [ 10 ] . Similarly, Wen et al. [ 11 ] used ultrasound imaging to guide needle insertion with respiratory gating. This method removes the need for patient cooperation in breath-holding protocols, but still requires a surgeon to complete the insertion or manually adjust it after the robotic system completes its measurements.

Furthermore, Schreiber et al. [ 12 , 13 , 14 ] and Zhang et al. [ 15 ] demonstrated a robotic arm capable of approaching and driving a needle insertion mechanism within the CT bore, eliminating the need to remove the patient from the CT bore for needle insertion. While this system showed potential to reduce operation time and radiation exposure by working directly within the CT bore, it lacks a precise mechanism to compensate for chest movement caused by the patient’s natural breathing cycle, limiting its potential to safely perform biopsy procedures.

In this work, we present an integrated sensing and control system capable of gated needle insertion within the CT bore. This system was developed to integrate into a CT-guided needle biopsy robot [ 14 ] , addressing current gaps in robot-assisted needle biopsy procedures.
Our contributions include:

A model for quiet breathing;

An integrated sensing and controls mechanism capable of working within the CT bore;

Autonomous four stage linear needle insertion procedure with gated controls;

Anomaly detection during autonomous procedure.

1. A model for quiet breathing;

2. An integrated sensing and controls mechanism capable of working within the CT bore;

3. Autonomous four stage linear needle insertion procedure with gated controls;

4. Anomaly detection during autonomous procedure.
